dcb@wdl1.wdl.loral.com (David C Blume) writes:

>wooledge@skybridge.SCL.CWRU.Edu (Gregory Wooledge) writes:
>>
>>To raise a ring's enchantment, throw it in a pile of lots of rings, and
>>zap the lot of them with a wand of polymorph.  Do this often enough, and the
>>laws of probability say that EVENTUALLY you will get what you want.
>
>I thought that when you poly-pile things, they KEPT their blessed/cursed
>state, and KEPT their enchantment.  (Wands still have as many zaps and
>weapons armor and rings maintain thier +'s amd -'s...

Wands do keep their charges, and armor and weapons maintain their enchantments.
However, rings don't always HAVE enchantments.  Consider the case of a -2 ring
of protection which is polymorphed into, say, a ring of hunger and then to a
+3 ring of protection.  The ring of hunger has no (numerical) enchantment, so
the "-2" isn't "remembered".

I haven't studied the game closely enough to know what happens when a ring of
protection is polymorphed DIRECTLY into another ring of protection, but I would
think that this would be rather uncommon.  And besides, if you don't like the
new enchantment of a RoP, just zap it a few more times, same as usual.


